 known: R(3,3) = 6, R(4,4) = 18, 43 <= R(5,5) <= 46, 102 <= R(6,6); tournament R(2..6) = 2, 4, 8, 14, 28, 34 <= R(7) <= 47 (THM-455)
== (4) the Seidel doubling D(G): omega and alpha laws ==
 all 1024 labeled graphs on 5 vertices: omega(D) - omega: {0: 331, 1: 487, 2: 200, 3: 5, 4: 1}; alpha(D) - alpha: {1: 818, 2: 200, 3: 5, 4: 1}; max(omega,alpha) increment: {0: 126, 1: 878, 2: 20}
 all 32768 labeled graphs on 6 vertices: omega(D) - omega: {0: 7742, 1: 18314, 2: 6090, 3: 615, 4: 6, 5: 1}; alpha(D) - alpha: {1: 26056, 2: 6090, 3: 615, 4: 6, 5: 1}; max(omega,alpha) increment: {0: 3016, 1: 27282, 2: 2470}
 Paley P_5: (omega, alpha) = (2, 2); D(P_5) on 10 vertices: (omega, alpha) = (3, 3) -> R(4,4) > 10 from the double
 Paley P_13: (omega, alpha) = (3, 3); D(P_13) on 26 vertices: (omega, alpha) = (4, 4) -> R(5,5) > 26 from the double
 Paley P_17: (omega, alpha) = (3, 3); D(P_17) on 34 vertices: (omega, alpha) = (5, 5) -> R(6,6) > 34 from the double
== (5) the orbit as a permutation tournament: Erdos-Szekeres ==
 n = 27: 42 odd values; LIS = 18, LDS = 9 (largest transitive subtournament of the permutation tournament = 18); Erdos-Szekeres guarantees a monotone subsequence of length 7
 n = 703: 63 odd values; LIS = 18, LDS = 17 (largest transitive subtournament of the permutation tournament = 18); Erdos-Szekeres guarantees a monotone subsequence of length 8
 n = 6171: 97 odd values; LIS = 17, LDS = 21 (largest transitive subtournament of the permutation tournament = 21); Erdos-Szekeres guarantees a monotone subsequence of length 10
 n = 77031: 130 odd values; LIS = 18, LDS = 32 (largest transitive subtournament of the permutation tournament = 32); Erdos-Szekeres guarantees a monotone subsequence of length 12
== (6) the exact law for the Seidel doubling: omega(D(G)) = largest induced complete split subgraph, alpha(D(G)) = max(alpha + 1, largest induced clique + independent set with no edges between) ==
 law verified on all labeled graphs with 3 <= n <= 6 (33864 graphs): True
 law verified on 300 random graphs with n = 7 and 120 with n = 8: True
 proof sketch: a clique of D(G) is a clique K of G on the plain copy plus an independent set I of G on the prime copy (the prime copy carries the complement), twins never adjacent, cross pairs adjacent iff adjacent in G; an independent set of D(G) is an independent I on the plain copy plus a clique K on the prime copy, cross pairs non-adjacent iff non-adjacent in G, twins allowed, which forces |K| = 1 whenever K meets I
 Paley P_5: complete-split number 3 = omega(D(P_5)); separated number 3, alpha(D(P_5)) = max(alpha + 1, sep) = 3 -> the double on 10 vertices gives R(4,4) > 10
 Paley P_13: complete-split number 4 = omega(D(P_13)); separated number 4, alpha(D(P_13)) = max(alpha + 1, sep) = 4 -> the double on 26 vertices gives R(5,5) > 26
 Paley P_17: complete-split number 5 = omega(D(P_17)); separated number 5, alpha(D(P_17)) = max(alpha + 1, sep) = 5 -> the double on 34 vertices gives R(6,6) > 34
 Paley P_29: complete-split number 5 = omega(D(P_29)); separated number 5, alpha(D(P_29)) = max(alpha + 1, sep) = 5 -> the double on 58 vertices gives R(6,6) > 58
 Paley P_37: complete-split number 7 = omega(D(P_37)); separated number 7, alpha(D(P_37)) = max(alpha + 1, sep) = 7 -> the double on 74 vertices gives R(8,8) > 74
